            <div xml:id="d459">

                <pb facs="0642" n="543" sameAs="frus:frus1936v05/pg543"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">422.11G93/1691: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Ecuador</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Gonzalez</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Quito</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1936-07-10T18:00:00-05:00">July 10, 1936—6
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received (July 11?)—10:15 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>30. Referring to my telegram No. 29, July 10, noon, the President informed me
                    that he today telegraphed Sherwell of the Manufacturers Trust Company to confer
                    immediately with the council and that he has suspended all proceedings pending
                    the results of the consultation. He added that he desires to reach an amicable
                    settlement and that he is convinced that this is feasible provided the
                    bondholders are willing to accept the best terms that Ecuador can offer.</p>
                <pb facs="0643" n="544" xml:id="pg544"/>
                <p>Undoubtedly the recent resignation of Avilés has removed the cause for concern as
                    to precipitate action. However, it has not lessened the determination of the
                    President to dispose of the question at this time on the basis of Ecuador’s
                    financial capacity. It is my opinion that the council should bear this in mind
                    in its discussions.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Gonzalez</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
